# =============================================================================
# HueyOS initramfs early-load module list
# =============================================================================
#
# Consumed by:
#   src/huey/kernel_build/initramfs_builder.py   (Step 17)
#   kernel/huey-linux/initramfs/modprobe.d/huey-v100.conf  (Step 16, for
#   binding policy, NOT for module selection)
#
# Format (initramfs-tools compatible):
#   - one module per line
#   - blank lines and lines beginning with '#' are ignored
#   - module options may follow the module name, space-separated
#     (e.g. "my_module opt=value"); the initramfs builder treats them as
#     positional modprobe arguments
#
# This file lists ONLY modules we deliberately want present in the
# initramfs. It is additive: Debian's initramfs-tools already autodetects
# the root filesystem driver, storage controllers, and most display
# drivers. If a module is already pulled in by initramfs-tools' own hooks,
# listing it here is redundant and should be avoided.
#
# Dependency resolution:
#   'modprobe' (and initramfs-tools' copy_modules_dir machinery) resolves
#   each module's 'modinfo -F depends' automatically. Do NOT add a module
#   here solely to satisfy another module's dependency: amdgpu, nvidia,
#   and nvidia-uvm each declare their own dependencies.
#
# Blacklist directives:
#   These belong in modprobe.d/huey-v100.conf (Step 16), NOT here. This
#   file never negates a driver; it only requests inclusion.
#
# Profile:  v100-workstation
# Target:   HueyOS custom kernel (LOCALVERSION=-huey1)
# =============================================================================

# --- Display path (AMD RX 570) -----------------------------------------------
# amdgpu drives the RX 570 display. Include it early so early KMS and
# Wayland bring-up can proceed from the initramfs. Do NOT blacklist or
# override amdgpu here; overrides belong in modprobe.d.
amdgpu

# --- Compute path (Tesla V100, proprietary 580.196.07) -----------------------
# nvidia and nvidia-uvm are the compute path. Including them in the
# initramfs ensures CUDA / NVML are ready as soon as userspace starts,
# without waiting for the udev cycle to load them.
#
# nvidia-modeset and nvidia-drm are intentionally NOT listed:
# the Tesla V100 has no display output, so neither is needed in the
# initramfs for the v100-workstation profile. If a future profile uses
# an NVIDIA GPU for display, add them there in that profile's
# modules.list.
nvidia
nvidia-uvm
